1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 g6 {Most Grunfeld and King's Indian players choose this system. The resulting positions are quite complex, and markedly different from those that arise after 3...Bf5, 3...e6, and 3...a6. We begin by solidifying our center and initiating a furious kingside assault.} 4. e3 {Black is at an early crossroads: the most popular move by a country mile is 4...Bg7, but Black has a few serious alternatives that must be studied carefully.} Bg7 {By far the most common response, completing the fianchetto.} (4... c6 {Although much less popular than 4...Bg7, this alternative must be taken seriously. Black solidifies his center and prepares an eventual queen sally to b6.} 5. h4 {As usual, we initiate kingside operations and pose Black with an early dilemma. Analogously to the position after 4...Bg7 5.h4, Black has two main ways to meet Harry's march: Black can continue developing with 5...Bg7 (transposing to a position that we analyze via the 4...Bg7 5.h4 c6 move order), or meet White at the top with 5...h5.} Bg7 (5... h5 6. Nf3 {Proceeding with development.} Bg7 (6... Bg4 {The early bishop sortie to g4 is very common, but the bishop will be tossed around by our pawns. We respond by unpinning our queen and reinforcing the idea of Ne5.} 7. Qd2 Nbd7 (7... Bxf3 {This exchange is squarely in White's favor: the resulting pawn structure offers White fantastic play in the center and on the kingside, as well as the bishop pair.} 8. gxf3 Bg7 9. e4 {Striking at the center without delay.} e6 10. O-O-O Nbd7 11. Kb1 {It is almost always worth investing a tempo to improve the safety of our king.} a6 12. Rg1 {Deploying the rook, and applying pressure on g6.} Ng8 {An ingenious move, opening up a line of fire to the h4-pawn. Since our lead in development is quite vast, we can afford to leave the h-pawn en prise!} 13. Bh3 Qxh4 (13... Ne7 14. Bg5 {This pin is quite nasty, since Black cannot afford to play ...f6 for obvious reasons.} O-O 15. Ne2 Nb6 16. Nf4 {Black is paying a steep price for advancing his h-pawn and opening the g-file: we threaten the devastating Nxh5 gxh5 Bf6, while Black's "attack" hasn't even gotten off the ground.} Nc4 17. Qb4 $18 {[%csl Re7] The knight is hanging, and Nxh5-Bf6 is imminent.}) 14. Rh1 {A cold-blooded response, sending the queen back where it belongs!} Qd8 15. exd5 {It is now time to smash open the center at all costs!} cxd5 16. Nxd5 $1 {At this point in your Jobava journey, moves like these should not elicit any surprise.} exd5 17. Rde1+ Ne7 (17... Kf8 18. Bd6+ $18) 18. Rxe7+ $1 {Another blow, removing the final defender from the e-file. Obviously, taking with the queen loses to Re1, so Black's king has no choice but to go on a walk.} Kxe7 19. Qb4+ {A surgical follow-up check, preventing ...Kf8.} Ke8 20. Re1+ $18) 8. Ng5 {This creative night sally underscores the awkwardness of the bishop on g4, which will provide fuel for a furious pawn storm in the center. f2-f3 and e3-e4 is coming, and Black can do absolutely nothing about it.} Bg7 9. f3 Bf5 10. e4 {In order to generate a suitable escape square for the bishop, Black must cede total control of the center.} dxe4 11. fxe4 Bg4 12. e5 Nh7 13. Nxh7 Rxh7 14. Ne4 $16) 7. Ne5 Nbd7 8. Bd3 Nxe5 (8... Qb6 $1 {This is the strongest move, but it is very infrequently played. Objectively, the position is dynamically equal, but there is no question that it is quite a bit easier to play White.} 9. a3 {An important resource, indirectly defending the b2-pawn.} O-O (9... Qxb2 $4 10. Na4 $18) 10. Na4 Qa5+ 11. Qd2 $1 {Lovely tactics! Black is posed with an ultimatum: take the knight and run the risk of losing their queen, or transition into a rather unpleasant endgame in which White's space advantage and better-placed pieces offers the better practical chances.} Qxa4 (11... Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Ng4 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Rhf1 $14 {[%cal Rf2f3,Rb2b4]}) 12. b3 {In order to extricate the queen, Black must come up with a series of computer moves.} Ne4 $8 13. Bxe4 Qa6 14. Bd3 Qb6 15. O-O {Do not be afraid to castle kingside even with the pawn on h4, since Black lacks the machinery and activity to exploit this cosmetic damage.} Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Bxe5 17. dxe5 {The pawn on e5 is incredibly nasty, since it controls important squares and severs the coordination between Black's pieces.} Qc7 18. Qc3 c5 19. e4 $1 {Sacrificing the e5-pawn for a furious initiative on both sides of the board.} d4 20. Qd2 Qxe5 21. f4 Qg7 22. b4 cxb4 (22... b6 23. f5 Bd7 24. e5 $1 gxf5 25. Bxf5 e6 26. Be4 Rac8 27. Rae1 $40 {[%cal Rf1f3,Rd2f4,Rf3g3]}) 23. Qxb4 $36) 9. Bxe5 O-O 10. Qf3 {Building up the kingside pressure.} Ng4 (10... Bg4 $6 11. Qg3 {[%cal Rf2f3] and White will send away the bishop on the next move.}) 11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. Qg3 {Setting up the threat of f2-f3, and virtually forcing Black to initiate a queen trade.} Qd6 (12... b5 13. f3 $1 {The e3-pawn is untouchable due to Qe5+, forcing Black's knight out of commission.} Nf6 14. Ne2 $14 {Our knight is ferried to f4, when the pressure against g6 will become seriously alarming. Black has nothing going on the other side of the board.}) 13. Qxd6 exd6 {The tactical battle has subsided, and it is time to switch gears to methodical positional play. The first step is to optimize our queenside pawn structure.} 14. a4 a5 15. b3 Nh6 16. Ne2 $14 {Step one has been achieved, and now it is time for step two: maneuvering the knight to f4, and opening up the queenside with c2-c4. White has a small but stable edge in the endgame. Following that, we will place our rook on b1 and exert nasty pressure on Black's b7-pawn. There is no question that White stands better, and we are playing essentially for two results.}) 6. Be2 h5 7. Nf3 {See 4...Bg7 5.h4 c6 6.Be2 h5 7.Nf3.}) (4... a6 {Combining ...g6 and ...a6 is rather awkward. Our response does not change.} 5. h4 Bg7 (5... h5 6. Nf3 Bg7 7. Ne5 O-O 8. Be2 {Straightforward and powerful: we threaten to smash open the kingside with g2-g4, which will likely lead to the summary execution of Black's monarch.} Nbd7 {Now, 9.g4 is hasty due to ...Nxe5 and ...Nxg4, so one more patient preparatory move is required.} 9. f3 Nxe5 10. Bxe5 c6 11. Qd2 {11.g4 was possible, but there is no hurry: we are in charge in the center, and Black is too passive and poorly coordinated to generate serious prospects on the queenside.} b5 12. O-O-O $40 {[%cal Rd1g1,Rg2g4] Our next move will Rdg1, when everything will finally be ready for g2-g4! Of course, 12...b4 merely exacerbates the issue due to 13.Na4, when Black will be saddled with additional queenside weaknesses.}) 6. h5 {Here, the exchange sacrifice is fully justified, since the d5-pawn will be undefended in the resulting position, costing Black a crucial tempo.} Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 {Highlighting the ineffectiveness of Black's fourth move. Black must now waste another tempo to defend the d5-pawn, giving us the window of opportunity that we need to develop long-lasting kingside pressure.} c6 (8... e6 9. Qh2 $1 {A crucial resource, aiming our queen-bishop battery at the c7 pawn. Black has no way to defend it without decisively compromising his king safety.} Nc6 (9... Kd7 10. Na4 $1 {[%cal Ra4c5]} b6 11. c4 {An old friend comes knocking! We are more familiar with the c2-c4 idea in a positional context, but it is just as powerful here. Opening up the c-file enables us to deploy our rook, when the pressure against c7 quickly becomes decisive.} dxc4 12. Rc1 {Now, ...b5 runs into the deadly Nc5+, so Black must part ways with the c4-pawn.} Bb7 13. Bxc4 Nc6 14. d5 $18 {It should be clear even to an untrained eye that White's attack is utterly crushing.}) 10. Bxc7 Qf6 {Having snagged the c7-pawn, we can now chill out and focus on bringing out the rest of our pieces. In particular, the knight is itching to occupy a certain deadly outpost on the kingside. We must listen to the horsey!} 11. Nh3 {[%cal Rh3f4,Rf4h5]} Bd7 12. Nf4 Qh6 13. Nh5 $18 {The knight on h5 is unassailable, and it controls the key f6 and g7-squares, restricting Black's already ultra-cramped position even further. We can continue by castling queenside, completing our development with Be2, and even finding appropriate digs for the other knight with Na4-b6.}) 9. Nf3 Nd7 {Had Black saved a tempo on ...c6, he would have been able to ferry his knight over to f6, forcing our queen out of h5 and essentially consolidating his material advantage. In this line, we beat him to the punch - with totally devastating effect.} 10. Ng5 {This and the next moves truly emphasize how deadly the queen + knight tandem can be in the right circumstances.} Rf8 11. Ne6 {[%cal Re6d8]} Qa5 12. Bc7 $1 $18 {[%cal Re6g7] Game, set, match!}) (4... c5 5. Nb5 Na6 6. c3 Bg7 7. h4 {Transposes to 4...Bg7 5.h4 c5 6.Nb5 Na6 7.c3.}) 5. h4 {This is not just a "bullet move," but a very dangerous system that confronts Black with serious problems. White's idea is to shove the pawn down to h5, when the resulting exchange sacrifice (i.e. ...Nxh5 Rxh5 gxh5 Qxh5) offers White a massive initiative, which can turn decisive if Black fails to reinforce the d5-pawn or unwisely decides to castle kingside. At a high level, Black almost always responds by pushing their own h-pawn one or two squares, but both of these options create long-term weaknesses on the kingside that we will use to spark the flames of an eventual kingside attack.} h5 {This is the most common response online, by quite a wide margin. Our next steps are to develop the knight and ready ourselves for queenside castling.} (5... O-O {Ignoring White's plan leads to a quick and decisive kingside attack. Amazingly, 5...0-0 is the second most common move online, with around 37,000 games at the time of analysis. A welcome statistic indeed!} 6. h5 $1 Nxh5 {Black takes the plunge.} (6... c5 {This is second in popularity behind 6...Nxh5, but objectively it is clearly Black's best try. Still, the resulting positions - with a wide open h-file granting us exciting attacking opportunities at every turn - are virtually impossible for a human to handle accurately.} 7. hxg6 hxg6 (7... fxg6 {This keeps the h-file closed, but creates a host of weaknesses in the center and on the kingside.} 8. dxc5 Nc6 9. Nf3 Bg4 (9... Qa5 10. Nd2 $1 {A nifty little resource, shuttling the knight to b3 and prying the queen away from the d5-pawn.} Qxc5 (10... e5 11. Nb3 Qd8 12. Bg5 Be6 13. Qd2 $16 {We will follow up by castling queenside, landing the d5 pawn in serious trouble. Obviously, Black cannot even dream of any compensation for the pawn!}) 11. Nb3 Qb6 (11... Qb4 12. a3 {This leads to the same position as after 11...Qb6, with the pawn on a3 clearly favoring White.}) 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Qxd5+ e6 14. Qe4 Bxb2 {Black has recovered the pawn, and the ensuing check on c3 may appear rather disconcerting, but Black's position remains virtually untenable due to the semi-open h-file, the lack of active prospects for the LSB, and the permanent weakness of the e6-pawn.} 15. Rd1 Bc3+ 16. Nd2 Rd8 17. Bd3 Qb4 18. Ke2 $1 {Unpinning the knight and preparing to trade on our own terms.} Bxd2 19. Rxd2 Qxe4 20. Bxe4 Rxd2+ 21. Kxd2 Bd7 22. Rb1 b6 23. Rb3 $14 {The bishop pair and our incredibly active rook guarantee us excellent winning chances in the endgame.})) 8. Qd2 {Rome was not built in a day! Before launching our kingside attack, we must do the bare minimum to complete our queenside development and shuttle our king to relative safety. With this move, we also take the sting out of ...cxd4, which will greatly increase the potency of the bishop sortie to h6.} Nc6 9. O-O-O Qa5 (9... Bf5 10. f3 {The standard response to Black's last move, underscoring the awkwardness of Black's bishop and preparing g2-g4, which will pave the way for our queen to land on the h-file.} Rc8 11. g4 {Beating Black to the punch!} Be6 12. Qh2 {Once the queen lands on h2, Black is totally doomed.} Re8 13. Nge2 {This patient move highlights the extent of our dominance. There is no need at all to hurry with Bh6; Black has absolutely nothing going on the queenside. Now, we are ready to thrust the bishop into h6 and follow up with Nf4, with utterly decisive consequences.} Qb6 14. Bh6 Bh8 15. Bf8 $1 {Beautiful!!} Kxf8 16. Qxh8+ Ng8 17. Rh7 Red8 {This position occurred in "Boknoy2019" - "Mustang6000", Lichess (blitz) 2021. White committed a small inaccuracy with Rg7, enabling Black to prolong the game with ...Ke8. Believe it or not, Black actually went on to win the game! Instead, immediate victory is yielded by a move on the other side of the board:} 18. Na4 $1 Qa5 19. Nxc5 Qxa2 20. Qg7+ Ke8 21. Qxg8# {The point of getting the knight to c5 was not to win a pawn, but to close off the king's only escape hatch!}) 10. a3 {One more bit of prophylaxis, taking the defensive burden off of our knight. Depending on Black's response, we can finally initiate kingside operations with Bh6 or casually finish our development with Nf3. Simply put, Black has very little to brag about on the queenside, and we have a lot more time on our hands than it may seem!} c4 11. Nf3 {An old friend comes knocking: we bring the knight over to e5, which will likely tip Black's already poorly-coordinated army over the edge.} b5 12. Ne5 Bb7 13. Qe1 $1 {Gorgeous maneuvering. We prepare f2-f3 and Qh4, when the attack will finally begin in earnest!} b4 14. axb4 {Black's queen is but a paper tiger, since 14...Qb1+ is cooly met with 15.Kd2 (followed by f2-f3, to eliminate the possibility of ...Ne4+).} Nxb4 15. f3 Qa1+ 16. Kd2 Qxb2 17. Rc1 {Cold-blooded and ridiculously powerful. Black's queenside activity has come to a shuddering halt, and now it is our time to shine!} Nh5 18. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 19. Qg3 {[%cal Gf4h6]} Kh8 20. Qh4 Na2 21. Nxa2 Qxa2 22. Qxh5+ Kg8 23. Bh6 {Removing the final obstacle. Without its last faithful defender, the Black king will be left entirely to its own devices. To use a phrase that both of us promised not to use in this course, the position plays itself!} Bxh6 24. Qxh6 Qa6 25. Qg5+ Kh8 26. Bxc4 $1 $18 {A simple but lovely tactic to top it all off. Rh1 is unstoppable, and mate follows in a few moves.}) (6... Bg4 $2 {This horrific move is incredibly common.} 7. f3 {It transpires that the pawn on h5 is untouchable due to g2-g4, trapping the bishop! Black has no choice but to retreat in shame.} Bf5 8. g4 Be6 9. Qd2 $18 {[%cal Rd2h2] Black's position is already resignable!}) 7. Rxh5 {The typical exchange sac works to perfection here, since the d5-pawn is undefended and the newly-castled king will get absolutely terrorized.} gxh5 8. Qxh5 {This is the best possible version of the exchange sac, since Black's king has found itself on the worst possible square. Our next step is to complete our development while leveling threat after threat against Black's hapless king.} c6 (8... e6 9. Bd3 f5 10. Qh2 $1 {Surgically precise. We saw this exact idea in the 4...a6 5.h4 Bg7 variation. The pawn on c7 represents Black's last hope to generate counterplay; without it, Black will be left without even an iota of play.} Na6 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. O-O-O {Having mangled Black's queenside, it turns out that we do not even need to waste any time capturing the pawn on c7! Instead, we can turn directly to the most important task at hand: getting the rook to h1 as quickly as possible.} c5 13. Nf3 $18 {Rh1 is coming, and Black's king is doomed.}) (8... Nd7 9. Bd3 Nf6 {Bringing the knight to f6 does not change the nature of the position in any way; the knight is a good defender, but it will be horribly outmatched.} 10. Qh2 c5 11. dxc5 {The point of this move is not so much to snag a pawn (we will give it right back) but to prevent ...c4, ensuring that our bishop remains singularly trained on the h7-pawn.} Qa5 12. O-O-O Qxc5 13. Nge2 a6 14. Rh1 h5 15. Ng3 $18 {Black will lose the h-pawn, and with it, any hopes of not getting checkmated before move 20!}) (8... f5 9. Nf3 Qe8 {This defensive approach is quite common online. Now, 10.Qh2 obviously maintains a decisive advantage, but we have a much simpler approach, cashing in our chips and attaining a decisive material advantage in the endgame.} 10. Qxe8 Rxe8 11. Nxd5 {The pawn on c7 cannot be defended!} Na6 12. Bxa6 bxa6 13. Nxc7 $18) 9. Bd3 f5 10. Nf3 Qe8 11. Qh2 Nd7 12. O-O-O Nf6 13. Rh1 h5 14. Ne5 Ng4 15. Qg3 Rf6 16. Qh4 Be6 17. Ne2 Nxe5 18. dxe5 Rg6 19. Qxh5 Rxg2 20. Qh7+ Kf8 21. Bg3 Qf7 22. Nf4 Rxg3 23. fxg3 $18) (5... h6 {A typical antidote to h4-h5: Black prepares ...g6-g5 in response. However, the h6-pawn constitutes a long-term weakness, and a timely h4-h5 - even if it fails to directly open up the kingside - will induce further weaknesses along the b1-h7 diagonal.} 6. Nf3 c6 7. Qd2 {Our approach is identical to the one we adopt in the main line (5...h5), quickly entrenching our knight on e5 and preparing to ferry our king to safety at a moment's notice.} O-O 8. Ne5 Nbd7 9. Bd3 Nxe5 10. Bxe5 {Black's position is cramped and unpleasant, and is powerless against the ensuing series of kingside pawn breaks.} Nd7 11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. h5 {Softening up Black's position.} g5 13. f4 $1 {Continuing to batter away at Black's kingside!} g4 14. f5 {Screwing in the pawn, leaving Black's bishop with no active prospects and the g4-pawn without a key defender.} Nf6 15. O-O-O b5 16. e4 b4 17. e5 bxc3 18. exf6+ exf6 19. Qxc3 $16 {[%cal Rc3c6,Rd1f1,Rh1h4] Black's position is riddled with holes. We can follow up with Rdf1 and Rh4, picking up the g4-pawn and proceeding to destroy Black on the kingside.}) (5... c6 {This move, although played orders of magnitude less frequently online than 5...h5 and 5...0-0, has been the choice of many top players, and for good reason. By supporting the d5-pawn, Black takes the sting out of the typical exchange sacrifice. In order to force Black to move his h-pawn, we must deploy our bishop to the somewhat awkward e2-square. Still, it is a worthy price to pay, for inducing ...h5 is crucial if we are to poke holes at Black's ultra-solid setup.} 6. Be2 {Now, h5 obviously becomes a serious threat. Black's response is virtually forced.} (6. h5 $5 {This is an interesting move that both of us have played, and it offers decent practical compensation for the exchange. However, objectively, there is no question that Black is significantly better with only a few accurate moves.} Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 Nd7 {The exchange sacrifice is far less effective here, since Black does not need to spend an additional tempo guarding the c6-pawn (compared to the same position in the 4...a6 line). Black uses this tempo to maneuver the knight to f6, dousing the flames of White's initiative. There is no question that White has good practical chances here, but we decided against reccommending this line, since White's compensation is objectively insufficient.}) 6... h5 7. Nf3 {The presence of the bishop on e2 does not substantively change the fabric of the position when compared to the main line (5...h5). Our plan remains the same, to quickly shepherd the knight to e5 and ultimately to chisel away at Black's kingside with g2-g4.} Bg4 8. Ne5 Bxe2 9. Qxe2 Nbd7 10. O-O-O {Obviously, we are only willing to trade on our own terms.} Nxe5 (10... Qa5 11. Kb1 {It is almost always worth throwing in this prophylactic move, which ensures that ...Ne4 will never cause us headaches.} Nxe5 (11... Rc8 {The fate that awaits Black after this careless move shows that Black cannot keep his king in the center forever.} 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. e4 {The Black monarch's days of restful solitude are over. The moment he escapes to the kingside, we will pry open that side of the board with g2-g4.} Nb6 14. Bg5 {Threatening exd5, and virtually forcing Black to permanently weaken the dark squares in the center.} e6 {Having induced these weaknesses, we now switch gears and force the queens off the board.} (14... O-O 15. g4 $40) 15. exd5 cxd5 16. Qb5+ Qxb5 17. Nxb5 {[%cal Rb5d6,Rb5a7] It transpires that Black is facing a rather problematic double-attack: there is no way to guard d6 and a7 at the same time! Black has no choice but to part ways with the corner pawn.} Kd7 18. Nxa7 Rc4 19. Be3 $14 {Black's drawing chances are significant, but we are obviously playing for two results. Note that Black has no way to cut off the knight's evacuation route, since ...Rb4 will always run into a3.}) 12. Bxe5 O-O-O {Black's king is safer here than on the kingside, but his main problems are now in the center.} 13. e4 Rhe8 14. f3 $16 {[%cal Rg2g4] White is more active and better coordinated. Our likeliest follow-up is g4, which is rather problematic for Black even though the king is on the other side of the board.}) 11. Bxe5 O-O (11... b5 {Without any support, the pawn storm is anything but scary.} 12. f3 {You should not bat an eye at this move by now, which simultaneously prepares g2-g4 and e2-e4.} Qa5 13. a3 {Taking the sting out of ...b4, which will now create a complex of weak squares on the queenside that we are in perfect position to exploit.} b4 14. axb4 Qxb4 15. Qa6 {An instructive move, showing that our play is not only limited to the center and kingside in this line. We use the attack on c6 to force Black's queen back to his side of the board, which in turn allows our queen to find an ideal perch on a3.} Qb6 (15... O-O 16. Qxc6 Rfc8 17. Qa6 Rxc3 18. bxc3 Qxc3 19. Rd3 $18 {Black's "attack" has fizzled out before it began.}) 16. Qa3 O-O 17. Na4 Qa5 18. g4 $1 {As is so customary in the Jobava London, we combine play on both sides of the board. Having taken the c5-square under firm control, we now turn our attention to the kingside, carrying out a typical sacrifical operation aimed at opening the h-file.} hxg4 19. h5 Nxh5 20. fxg4 Nf6 21. Qxe7 {The queen joins the party, hitting Black where it hurts.} Qd8 (21... Rfe8 $2 22. Qxf6 $3 {Who needs the queen anyway?} Rxe5 23. Qxc6 Ree8 24. Rdf1 Rac8 25. Qd7 Rf8 26. Rxf7 $1 $18) 22. Qxd8 Raxd8 23. Bxf6 Bxf6 24. Kd2 $14 {[%cal Rd1a1,Ra4c5] We can finally see the importance of placing the knight on a4. Having done our job on the kingside, we will once again switch our attention to the other side of the board, deploying our rook to the a-file (it is especially good on a3, where it lends additional support to the e3-pawn) and entrenching the knight on c5 when the time is right.}) 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. g4 $1 $40 {Opening up a devastating kingside attack.} hxg4 14. Rdg1 $5 Kg7 15. Qxg4 Qc8 16. Qg2 {White will develop the attack with a well-timed h5, as well as the knight transfer to f4 via e2. Meanwhile, Black has absolutely nothing going on the other side of the board.}) (5... c5 {Yet again, this move is bad by default, since it allows White to establish a knight on b5 and follow up by securing the d4-pawn.} 6. Nb5 Na6 7. c3 O-O 8. Be2 {Developing the bishop and preparing h5.} Qb6 9. a4 Bd7 10. h5 $14 {Black's queenside counterplay has ground to a halt, while we are just getting started on the other flank. We will follow up by trading on g6 and maneuvering the knight to e5.}) 6. Nf3 O-O (6... c5 {Our response to this move is one and the same!} 7. Nb5 Na6 8. c3 O-O 9. Be2 {In contrast to 5...h5, we will have a harder time engineering a kingside attack, but from a strategic perspective, our advantage is indisputable.} Bg4 10. O-O Qb6 11. a4 $14 {Supporting the knight and taking the sting out of ...Bxf3. Black can barely move a muscle, e.g.} Rac8 $2 12. a5 Qd8 13. Nxa7 Ra8 14. Nb5 $16) (6... Bg4 7. Qd2 Bxf3 {This is the only way to "justify" the bishop's development to g4, but the resulting pawn structure works squarely in our favor.} 8. gxf3 {White's pawn structure may be visually unappealing, but the pawn on f3 will help prepare e4, and the semi-open g-file works in our favor as well.} O-O 9. O-O-O c5 {The only reasonable attempt to muddy the waters, though it falls woefully short.} (9... c6 10. e4 e6 11. Rg1 {Our last two moves underscore the strategic error inherent in the trade on f3.} Nbd7 12. e5 Nh7 13. Bd3 {Everything is now in place for the decisive breakthrough.} c5 14. Bxg6 $1 fxg6 15. Rxg6 Kh8 16. Rdg1 Rf7 17. Nb5 {White's knight joins in from the other side. Once it reaches d6, Black will be unable to deal with the countless threats coming from every side.} Qxh4 18. Bg3 {Calm, cool, and collected! It is important to shoo Black's queen away from the kingside before reopening the g-file.} Qe7 19. Nd6 Rxf3 20. Bf4 $1 {Imprisoning Black's rook and reopening the g-file.} Rg8 21. Bh6 $18) 10. dxc5 $1 {With the light-squared bishop gone, Black is unable to sustain his center without creating additional weaknesses.} e6 (10... Qa5 $2 11. Nxd5 {Intrepid and powerful!} Qxa2 {This move is almost never as scary as it looks; we disarm it by vacating an escape route for our king.} 12. Nxe7+ Kh7 13. Qb4 $18 {The check on a1 is innocuous, and we can always bring our bishop to d3 to clog up the d-file.}) 11. Bd6 {Hitting Black where it hurts!} Re8 12. e4 $18 {In the words of my coach, White has compensation for his extra pawn :)}) (6... c6 7. Ne5 {See 4...c6 5.h4 h5 6.Nf3 Bg7 7.Ne5.}) 7. Qd2 {Preparing to castle kingside.} c5 (7... Bg4 8. Ne5 Nbd7 9. f3 Bf5 10. O-O-O c5 11. g4 $1 hxg4 12. fxg4 Nxg4 13. Nxg4 Bxg4 14. Be2 Bxe2 15. Qxe2 $16 {[%cal Rh4h5]}) 8. Ne5 {This centralizing move is far more accurate than the immediate 8.0-0-0, which would enable Black to generate troublesome queenside counterplay with ...Qa5.} Nc6 {This position has occurred countless times in high-level tournament practice. Our first step is to control the e4-square and restrict the mobility of the f6-knight.} 9. f3 {A crucial follow-up, controlling the e4-square and preparing g2-g4.} cxd4 (9... Bf5 10. Bb5 Nb4 (10... cxd4 11. exd4 {See 9...cxd4 10.exd4 Bf5 11.Bb5.}) 11. Ba4 {We are used to this idea with colors reversed (i.e. ...Bb4-Ba5 in response to Nb5). Black's superficial queenside play has been fully disarmed, and we proceed to push the knight back to c6.} cxd4 12. exd4 Rc8 13. a3 Nc6 (13... Na6 14. O-O-O Qa5 15. Bb3 b5 16. Kb1 b4 17. Ne2 $18) 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Na4 {As usual, we assert our dominance on both sides of the board. By controlling c5, we permanently halt Black's queenside play. The stage is now set for a lightning kingside offensive.} Ne8 16. g4 hxg4 17. h5 $40 {The computer assigns White a relatively modest plus, but from a human perspective, Black's position is nearly hopeless. Black has no good answer to the manifold threats on the kingside, e.g.} Nf6 18. h6 $1 Bh8 19. h7+ $1 Nxh7 20. fxg4 Be4 21. O-O-O $1 {A final flourish, connecting the rooks; we only need one of the towers to checkmate black on the h-file.} Bxh1 22. Rxh1 Bg7 23. Qh2 Nf6 24. Nc5 {with the unstoppable threat of Ned7, which will remove the only defender of the h7-square and lead to quick checkmate. This line is worth reviewing several times, for it clearly illustrates our ability to dominate on both sides of the board. It is worth thinking of the queenside as the "positional side," to be used primarily for the sake of optimizing the placement of the "Jobava knight." The h-file, on the other hand, is used to do the checkmating!}) 10. exd4 Bf5 (10... Qb6 {This tempting move plays right into our hands.} 11. O-O-O {Shepherding the king to safety and protecting b2 and d4. Black has no good answer to the typical plan of g2-g4 and h4-h5, which will tear open the kingside and quickly lead to a decisive attack.} Bf5 12. g4 (12. Nxc6 $5 {It is also possible for White to play it slow.} bxc6 (12... Qxc6 13. Re1 e6 14. Be5 $16) 13. Na4 $16 {Asserting dominance over the c5-square. Note that Black has absolutely no potential to develop a queenside attack, since the b8-square is permanently under our control and our last move renders ...c6-c5 impossible.}) 12... hxg4 13. h5 {While the engine finds a way to prolong the game, all human moves lead to Black's rapid demise.} Nxh5 (13... gxh5 14. fxg4 hxg4 15. Bd3 {Clinical precision! Trading off the light-squared bishop decisively loosens Black's control over h7.} Bxd3 16. Nxd3 {This recapture makes a lot of sense when you keep in mind that the queen's optimal square is on h2.} Nxd4 17. Ne5 Nf5 18. Qh2 $18 {We will follow up with Rdg1 and Nxg4. Black's days are numbered!}) (13... gxf3 14. h6 Bh8 15. h7+ {Prying open the h-file in thematic fashion. 15...Kg7 merely hastens Black's demise after 16.Bh6+, so he has no choice but to partake of the forbidden fruit.} Nxh7 16. Nxd5 Qd8 17. Qh2 g5 18. Bd3 $1 $18 {Just as in the 13...gxh5 line, this move is an integral part of White's attacking strategy. Eliminating the final defender of the h7-square spells immediate doom.}) (13... Nxe5 14. Bxe5 Nxh5 {This is Black's most resilient continuation from an objective point of view, but it leads to a position that we can only refer to as indefensible.} 15. Nxd5 Qd8 16. Rxh5 $1 {Pow! In contrast to the other lines within the 10...Qb6 sub-variation, the decisive invasion will occur along the g-file.} gxh5 17. Qg5 Bg6 18. Nxe7+ Kh7 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 {It may seem that White's attack has ground to a halt, but we are actually just one short step away from setting up a mating net.} 20. Bc4 {Calm developing moves like this one should no longer come as a surprise to you. This sets up the virtually unstoppable threat of Nf5+, when ...Kg8 is met with Qxg6+ (that is why we need the services of the bishop!).} Kh7 (20... Rh8 21. fxg4 hxg4 22. Rf1 $18 {[%cal Rf1f7]}) 21. Rh1 {Black is checkmated seven ways to sunday, e.g.} Qxd4 22. Nf5 $1 $18 {The computer's top suggestion here is 22...Qg1+, which tells us all that we need to know about the position!}) 14. fxg4 Nxe5 15. gxf5 Nf3 16. Qg2 Nxf4 17. Qxf3 g5 18. Rg1 Qf6 19. Qg4 $40 {Black has succeeded in staving off checkmate for the time being, but his position remains untenable from a practical standpoint. The following line demonstrates the truth of this statement:} Rac8 20. Kb1 {Given the total lack of counterplay on the queenside, we are well within our rights to optimize the safety of our king before rolling up our sleeves on the other side of the board.} a6 21. Qxg5 Qxg5 22. Rxg5 {It may seem counterintuitive to trade off the queens, but we have plenty of pieces with which to finish Black off on the kingside.} Kh7 23. Rg4 Nh5 24. Rh4 $18 {[%cal Rf1e2] Black loses the h5-knight, and with it, the game.}) 11. Bb5 $1 {A very interesting and unusual idea that poses serious practical problems. As is the custom all throughout the 3...g6 variation, White combines positional and tactical play on the two flanks: on the queenside, we wish to trade on c6 and control the c5-square with Na4. On the kingside, we are awaiting the perfect opportunity to blast open the landscape with g2-g4 and h4-h5. Black can maintain the balance with perfect computer play, but from a practical standpoint, the position is virtually unplayable.} Nxe5 (11... Qb6 {This move is no better than it was on the previous move. In fact, it merely encourages us to do what we wanted to do anyway: assert our dominance over the c5-square.} 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Na4 Qb5 14. b3 {A familiar situation: Black has run out of active ideas on the queenside, and the stage is set for decisive kingside action.} Nd7 15. Nxd7 Bxd7 16. Nc5 Bf5 {The tempting 17.g4 is possible, but we have at our disposal a far more powerful - and much more beautiful - idea.} 17. Bc7 {A picturesque position that is worth examining for more than a few seconds! Yes, your suspicion is correct - Black's queen is trapped in the middle of the queenside, and he has absolutely no good answer to the threat of a2-a4.} a5 18. a4 Qb4 19. c3 $18 {It is rather amazing that we win the game without making a single attacking move on the kingside. Such is the unique power of the Jobava London!}) (11... Rc8 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Na4 {At this point, you should be ready to execute the last two moves automatically. With the c5-square under complete control, the stage is set for decisive action on the kingside.} Nd7 (13... Ne8 14. g4 hxg4 15. O-O-O f6 (15... Bxe5 16. Bxe5 f6 17. fxg4 Bxg4 18. Rdg1 Bh5 19. Nc5 fxe5 20. Ne6 Qd6 21. Nxf8 Kxf8 22. Qh6+ Kf7 $8 (22... Kg8 23. Qxh5 $18) 23. dxe5 Qxe5 24. Re1 $1 Qg7 25. Rhf1+ Kg8 26. Qxg7+ Nxg7 27. Rxe7 $36) 16. Nxg4 $40) 14. Nxd7 Qxd7 15. Nc5 Qe8 (15... Qd8 16. O-O-O Qb6 17. Rde1 Rfe8 18. g4 hxg4 19. fxg4 Bxg4 20. h5 $1 gxh5 21. Reg1 e5 22. Bh6 exd4 23. Qg5 $18) 16. O-O-O $16) 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. O-O-O {It may seem brazen to give up the e5-pawn, but our development is our top priority. Furthermore, once Black takes a bite of the forbidden apple, we swiftly transition our initiative to the center.} Nxe5 (13... Qb6 14. Bxd7 Bxd7 15. Nxd5 Qe6 {Here, do not walk into 16.Nc7?? Qxa2 17.Qxd7 Rad8 and White loses instantly!} 16. Nxe7+ Qxe7 17. Qxd7 $18) 14. Rhe1 Nc6 {A critical position has been reached. The natural move is, of course, 15.Nxd5, but that allows Black to force the queens off the board with 15...e6. Instead, we seize the opportunity to force the dark-squared bishops off the board, rendering Black's monarch even more susceptible to all sorts of shenanigans.} 15. Bh6 Bxh6 (15... e5 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Nxd5 Nd4 (17... Qxh4 18. g4 Be6 (18... hxg4 19. Rh1 $18 {[%cal Rd2h6]}) 19. Rh1 Qd8 20. gxh5 Bxd5 21. hxg6 $1 fxg6 22. Qh6+ Kf7 23. Bd3 $1 Qf6 24. Bc4 Bxc4 25. Qh7+ Qg7 (25... Ke6 26. Qd7#) 26. Rd7+ Ne7 27. Rxe7+ Kxe7 28. Qxg7+ $18) 18. Rxe5 $1 {A scintillating piece sacrifice.} Nxb5 19. Qf4 Be6 $8 (19... Qa5 20. Rxf5 $1 gxf5 21. Qg5+ Kh8 22. Qh6+ Kg8 23. Nf6#) (19... Qc8 20. Rd2 Re8 21. g4 Be6 (21... hxg4 22. fxg4 Qb8 23. Rde2 Rxe5 24. Rxe5 Be6 25. Qf6+ Kh7 26. Rxe6 fxe6 27. Qf7+ Kh6 28. Nf4 $1 Qg8 29. g5#) 22. Qf6+ Kg8 23. Rxh5 $3 gxh5 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Qh6+ Kg8 26. Nf6#) 20. Nc3 Nxc3 21. Rxd8 Nxa2+ 22. Kb1 Raxd8 23. Rxe6 fxe6 24. Qe5+ Kh7 25. Kxa2 $14) (15... e6 {This fails to a lovely tactical sequence that is worth memorizing:} 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Bxc6 bxc6 18. g4 $1 hxg4 19. fxg4 {The point of White's entire strategy is on full display in the event of 19...Bxg4, which runs into 20.Qd4+ - that is why it was so important to trade the bishops. Black has no choice but to accept a miserable endgame.} Be4 20. Qd4+ Qf6 21. Nxe4 dxe4 22. g5 {Clamping down on the kingside and forcing the queen trade on our terms.} Qxd4 23. Rxd4 $14 {We will recover the e4-pawn on the next move, when Black's deficient queenside pawn structure, coupled with the superior activity of our rooks, will grant us excellent winning chances.}) 16. Qxh6 e6 17. g4 $1 {Boom shaka-laka! The point of this move, believe it or not, is not to follow up with h5, but rather to open up the knight's access to e4 and g5.} hxg4 18. fxg4 Bxg4 19. Ne4 $1 {The point! The threat of Ng5 is absolutely devastating.} Qe7 (19... Re8 20. h5 Bxh5 (20... gxh5 21. Nf6+ $18) 21. Be2 Qh4 22. Nd6 Red8 23. Rh1 Qf6 24. Bxh5 Rxd6 25. Bxg6 $1 fxg6 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Rdf1 $18) (19... Bxd1 20. Ng5 $18) 20. Ng5 f6 21. Nxe6 Bxe6 22. Rxe6 Qxe6 23. Qxg6+ Kh8 24. Qh5+ Kg7 25. Rg1+ $18 *

